      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The graft of choice for the left anterior descending coronary artery is the left internal mammary artery because of superior long-term patency. However, controversy exists regarding the graft of choice for the right coronary artery and for the posterior descending branch."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "METHODS AND RESULTS",
          "content": "Two types of pedicled arterial grafts were used for the right coronary and the posterior descending arteries in patients undergoing coronary bypass surgery between January 1991 and September 1994. Group A comprised 114 patients with a right internal mammary artery (RIMA) graft, and group B consisted of 127 patients with an in situ right gastroepiploic artery (R-GEA) graft. Mean age was 56.9 years in group A and 63.3 years in group B; 7.9% (9 of 114) and 33.9% (43 of 127) were diabetics in groups A and B, respectively. Overall mortality was 2.6% (3 deaths) for group A and 3.9% (5 deaths) for group B (P = NS). However, the prevalence of perioperative myocardial infarction in the right coronary artery distribution was significantly higher for group A (5.3%, or 6 of 114) than for group B (0.8%, or 1 of 127; P < .05), and the reoperation rate for graft failure (from 0 to 12 months after surgery) was significantly higher for the RIMA (4.4%, or 5 of 114) than for the R-GEA (0%; P < .05). Also, the prevalence of deep sternal wound infection in diabetics was significantly higher in group A (22.2%, or 2 of 9) than in group B (4.6%, or 2 of 43; P < .05)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Our preliminary results suggest that the failure rate of the RIMA graft is significantly higher, especially if used as a pedicled graft to the posterior descending artery. The risk of sternal wound complications is greater in diabetics if both internal mammary arteries are used for grafting. Therefore, the R-GEA graft is preferred in diabetics and whenever the posterior descending artery is the target vessel."
        }
      ]},
